## Yes – you can draw contour lines **directly from a `raster-dem` source at runtime**  
MapLibre GL JS does not have a built-in “contour” layer type, but the **`maplibre-contour`** library does the heavy lifting for you:

* It reads the same DEM tiles you already use for hillshade / 3-D terrain.  
* In a Web-Worker it extracts elevation values, runs a marching-squares algorithm, and serves the result as a **virtual vector source** (via a custom URL protocol).  
* You then add a normal `line` (or `symbol`) layer that points at that vector source – exactly the same way you would use any other vector tileset.

Below is a step-by-step guide, a complete working example, and a few gotchas to keep in mind.

---

## 1️⃣ Install the library

```bash
# npm / yarn / pnpm
npm install maplibre-contour   # or: yarn add maplibre-contour
```

If you are loading scripts via `<script>` tags, you can pull the UMD bundle from a CDN:

```html
<script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/maplibre-contour@latest/dist/maplibre-contour.min.js"></script>
```

> **Note** – `maplibre-contour` works with MapLibre GL JS ≥ 2.0 (the version that ships the `color-relief` and `hillshade` layers). Make sure your MapLibre version matches the library’s peer-dependency.

---

## 2️⃣ Create (or reuse) a `raster-dem` source

Your DEM must be served in a format that the library can decode. The most common encodings are:

| Encoding | How the elevation is packed | Typical source URL pattern |
|----------|----------------------------|----------------------------|
| `mapbox` | 16-bit signed integer (meters) stored in the red/green channels | `https://example.com/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.png` |
| `terrarium` | 24-bit unsigned integer (meters + 0.1 m) stored in RGB | `https://example.com/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.webp` |
| `raw` | 32-bit float (meters) in a single channel (rare) | `…` |

```js
// Add the DEM source once – you’ll reuse it for terrain, hillshade, etc.
map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  url: 'https://tiles.mapterhorn.com/{z}/{x}/{y}.webp', // <-- your DEM tiles
  tileSize: 256,
  // IMPORTANT: tell MapLibre the encoding so it can read the values correctly
  // (this is also required by maplibre-contour)
  encoding: 'terrarium'   // or 'mapbox' depending on your tiles
});
```

> **Why `encoding` matters** – The DEM tiles are just raw pixel data. If MapLibre (or the contour worker) interprets them with the wrong scheme you’ll get wildly wrong elevations (or flat contours).

---

## 3️⃣ Spin up a `DemSource` from `maplibre-contour`

```js
import mlcontour from 'maplibre-contour';

// 1️⃣ Create the helper object – give it the same URL pattern and encoding
const demSource = new mlcontour.DemSource({
  url: 'https://tiles.mapterhorn.com/{z}/{x}/{y}.webp', // same as the raster-dem source
  encoding: 'terrarium',   // must match the source above
  maxzoom: 12,             // highest zoom you expect to request contours for
  // Optional: run the worker in a separate thread (recommended for UI smoothness)
  worker: true
});

// 2️⃣ Register the helper with the MapLibre instance – this adds the custom protocol
demSource.setupMaplibre(maplibregl);
```

### What `setupMaplibre` does

* Registers a **protocol handler** (`mlcontour://…`) that the map can request as a vector tile URL.  
* Starts a Web-Worker (if `worker:true`) that will decode DEM tiles, run the contour algorithm, and return GeoJSON-like vector tiles on-the-fly.

---

## 4️⃣ Add a *virtual* vector source that points at the contour protocol

```js
// The URL you pass here is *not* a real HTTP endpoint – it’s a protocol that the
// DemSource object knows how to answer.
map.addSource('contours', {
  type: 'vector',
  // The protocol URL is built by the helper; you can pass options that affect
  // the generated contours (see step 5 for the full list).
  tiles: [
    demSource.contourProtocolUrl({
      // ── Contour generation options ────────────────────────────────────────
      // Elevation value that will be stored in each feature (default: 'ele')
      elevationKey: 'ele',
      // Property name for the contour level (1 = major, 0 = minor)
      levelKey: 'level',
      // The name of the source-layer that will appear in the vector tiles
      contourLayer: 'contours',
      // Zoom-dependent spacing (in meters) for minor / major lines
      thresholds: {
        // zoom: [minorSpacing, majorSpacing]
        11: [200, 1000],
        12: [100, 500],
        14: [50, 200]
      }
    })
  ],
  // The vector tiles are generated on-the-fly, so we can set a generous maxzoom.
  maxzoom: 15
});
```

### `thresholds` explained

| Zoom | Minor interval | Major interval |
|------|----------------|----------------|
| 11   | 200 m          | 1000 m         |
| 12   | 100 m          | 500 m          |
| 14   | 50 m           | 200 m          |

*Minor* contours are drawn thinner (or with a different style) than *major* ones.  
You can omit `thresholds` – the library will fall back to a default set (≈ 100 m minor, 500 m major).

---

## 5️⃣ Add a `line` layer (or a `symbol` layer for labels)

```js
// Simple contour lines
map.addLayer({
  id: 'contour-lines',
  type: 'line',
  source: 'contours',
  'source-layer': 'contours',   // matches the `contourLayer` name above
  paint: {
    // Use the `level` property to differentiate major/minor lines
    'line-color': 'rgba(0,0,0,0.6)',
    'line-width': [
      'match',
      ['get', 'level'],
      1, 1.2,   // major (level === 1) → thicker
      0, 0.6,   // minor (level === 0) → thinner
      0.4       // fallback
    ]
  }
});
```

#### Optional: label the major contours

```js
map.addLayer({
  id: 'contour-labels',
  type: 'symbol',
  source: 'contours',
  'source-layer': 'contours',
  filter: ['==', ['get', 'level'], 1],   // only major lines
  layout: {
    'symbol-placement': 'line',
    'text-field': ['to-string', ['get', 'ele']], // elevation value
    'text-font': ['Open Sans Bold', 'Arial Unicode MS Bold'],
    'text-size': 12,
    'text-allow-overlap': false,
    'text-ignore-placement': false
  },
  paint: {
    'text-color': '#222',
    'text-halo-color': '#fff',
    'text-halo-width': 1
  }
});
```

> **Tip** – The `elevationKey` you chose (`'ele'` in the example) is the property you read with `['get', 'ele']`. If you need feet instead of meters, convert it in the expression (`['*', ['get', 'ele'], 3.28084]`).

---

## 6️⃣ Full minimal example (HTML + JS)

```html
<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre runtime contours</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, #map { margin:0; height:100vh; width:100vw; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/maplibre-contour@latest/dist/maplibre-contour.min.js"></script>
<script>
  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣ Initialise the map
  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {},
      layers: []   // we’ll add everything programmatically
    },
    center: [-122.447303, 37.753574],
    zoom: 12,
    pitch: 45,          // optional – you can see terrain if you also enable it
    antialias: true
  });

  map.on('load', () => {
    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    // 2️⃣ Add the DEM source (used later for terrain/hillshade too)
    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
      type: 'raster-dem',
      url: 'https://tiles.mapterhorn.com/{z}/{x}/{y}.webp',
      tileSize: 256,
      encoding: 'terrarium'   // <-- must match your tiles
    });

    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    // 3️⃣ (Optional) enable 3-D terrain so you can see the DEM in 3-D
    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1 });

    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    // 4️⃣ Set up maplibre-contour
    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    const demSource = new mlcontour.DemSource({
      url: 'https://tiles.mapterhorn.com/{z}/{x}/{y}.webp',
      encoding: 'terrarium',
      maxzoom: 12,
      worker: true
    });
    demSource.setupMaplibre(maplibregl);

    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    // 5️⃣ Add the virtual vector source that serves contour tiles
    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    map.addSource('contours', {
      type: 'vector',
      tiles: [
        demSource.contourProtocolUrl({
          elevationKey: 'ele',
          levelKey: 'level',
          contourLayer: 'contours',
          thresholds: {
            11: [200, 1000],
            12: [100, 500],
            14: [50, 200]
          }
        })
      ],
      maxzoom: 15
    });

    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    // 6️⃣ Draw the contour lines
    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'contour-lines',
      type: 'line',
      source: 'contours',
      'source-layer': 'contours',
      paint: {
        'line-color': 'rgba(0,0,0,0.6)',
        'line-width': [
          'match',
          ['get', 'level'],
          1, 1.2,
          0, 0.6,
          0.4
        ]
      }
    });

    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    // 7️⃣ (Optional) label major contours
    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'contour-labels',
      type: 'symbol',
      source: 'contours',
      'source-layer': 'contours',
      filter: ['==', ['get', 'level'], 1],
      layout: {
        'symbol-placement': 'line',
        'text-field': ['to-string', ['get', 'ele']],
        'text-font': ['Open Sans Bold', 'Arial Unicode MS Bold'],
        'text-size': 12,
        'text-allow-overlap': false
      },
      paint: {
        'text-color': '#222',
        'text-halo-color': '#fff',
        'text-halo-width': 1
      }
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Open the file in a browser and you’ll see contour lines appear instantly as you pan/zoom. No pre-generated vector tiles are required.

---

## 7️⃣ Things to watch out for

| Issue | Why it happens | Fix / Mitigation |
|-------|----------------|------------------|
| **Wrong elevations / flat contours** | DEM `encoding` mismatched (e.g., you tell the library it’s `terrarium` but the tiles are `mapbox`). | Verify the source’s `encoding` property and pass the same string to `DemSource`. |
| **Performance hiccups on low-end devices** | The worker must decode DEM tiles and run marching-squares for every tile that appears in the viewport. | • Keep `maxzoom` as low as possible (contours above that zoom are just up-scaled). <

Below is a **complete, copy-and-paste ready** recipe that turns the empty “page-background” you see above the horizon into a real sky with a gentle haze that blends into the terrain.

> **What you need**
> 1. A `raster-dem` source that you already use for `map.setTerrain()`.
> 2. The map must be **pitched** (you already have `pitch: 70`).
> 3. A **sky object** (or `map.setSky()`) – this is the only way MapLibre draws a sky.  
>    Mapbox-only properties such as `sky-type`, `sky-atmosphere-sun`, etc. do nothing in MapLibre.

---

## 1. Add the sky (root-level style object)  

You can either **declare it in the style JSON** or **call `map.setSky()`** after the map has loaded.  
Both approaches are equivalent – pick the one you prefer.

### A. Declaring the sky in the style JSON  

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "sources": {
    "terrain-dem": {
      "type": "raster-dem",
      "url": "https://tiles.mapterhorn.com/tilejson.json",
      "tileSize": 256,
      "maxzoom": 12
    },
    "satellite": {
      "type": "raster",
      "url": "https://tiles.example.com/sat/{z}/{x}/{y}.png",
      "tileSize": 256
    }
  },

  "layers": [
    {
      "id": "satellite",
      "type": "raster",
      "source": "satellite"
    }
    // …your other layers (hillshade, labels, etc.)
  ],

  /*  ←  THIS IS THE SKY OBJECT  →  */
  "sky": {
    "sky-color": "#199EF3",          // deep blue at zenith
    "horizon-color": "#ffffff",     // bright near the horizon
    "sky-horizon-blend": 0.5,        // how quickly the blue fades to the horizon colour
    "horizon-fog-blend": 0.4,        // how much fog mixes in at the horizon
    "fog-color": "#cce6ff",          // very light blue-white fog
    "fog-ground-blend": 0.3          // how far the fog reaches down onto the terrain
  }
}
```

> **Why these numbers?**  
> * `sky-color` + `horizon-color` give you a classic “blue-sky-to-white-horizon” gradient.  
> * `sky-horizon-blend` ≈ 0.5 makes the transition smooth but still noticeable.  
> * `horizon-fog-blend` ≈ 0.4 adds a subtle haze right at the horizon line.  
> * `fog-color` is a very light blue-white; you can make it a bit pinkish for sunrise/sunset.  
> * `fog-ground-blend` ≈ 0.3 lets the fog cling to the lower slopes without drowning the whole scene.

### B. Adding the sky with JavaScript (`map.setSky()`)

If you prefer to keep the style JSON untouched, just call `setSky` after the map is ready:

```javascript
// -------------------------------------------------
// 1️⃣  Create the map (you already have this)
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: {
    version: 8,
    sources: {
      'terrain-dem': {
        type: 'raster-dem',
        url: 'https://tiles.mapterhorn.com/tilejson.json',
        maxzoom: 12
      },
      // …other sources (imagery, vector, …)
    },
    layers: [
      // …your layers (raster, hillshade, symbols, etc.)
    ]
  },
  center: [13.4, 52.5],
  zoom: 12,
  pitch: 70,          // you already have this
  bearing: 0,
  antialias: true    // recommended for 3-D
});

// -------------------------------------------------
// 2️⃣  Wait for the style to load
map.on('load', () => {
  // 3️⃣  Enable terrain (you already do this)
  map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1 });

  // 4️⃣  Add the sky
  map.setSky({
    'sky-color': '#199EF3',
    'horizon-color': '#ffffff',
    'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,
    'horizon-fog-blend': 0.4,
    'fog-color': '#cce6ff',
    'fog-ground-blend': 0.3
  });
});
```

> **Tip:** `map.setSky()` can be called **any time after the map has loaded** – you can even change the colours on the fly (e.g. day/night switch).

---

## 2. Make sure the sky isn’t hidden

* **Background layer** – The background layer is drawn **under** the sky, so a solid `background-color` will not cover the sky.  
  If you have a background layer with `background-color: "#fff"` you can keep it; the sky will appear on top.
* **Opaque raster layers** – If you have a full-screen raster layer (e.g. a satellite image) that covers the whole viewport, it will also be drawn **under** the sky, so you’re fine.
* **Order of operations** – The sky is rendered **after** the background and **before** terrain, so you don’t need to worry about layer ordering.

---

## 3. Optional: Tweak the look

| Property                | Typical range | What it does |
|-------------------------|---------------|--------------|
| `sky-color`             | `#0a2e5b` → `#6ab7ff` | Base colour at the zenith. Darker = night-time, lighter = midday. |
| `horizon-color`         | `#ffffff` → `#ffddaa` | Colour right at the horizon line. |
| `sky-horizon-blend`     | `0.2` → `0.8` | How quickly the sky colour fades to the horizon colour. Smaller = sharper line. |
| `horizon-fog-blend`     | `0.0` → `0.6` | Amount of fog mixed in at the horizon. |
| `fog-color`             | any CSS colour | Colour of the haze that “sticks” to the terrain. |
| `fog-ground-blend`      | `0.0` → `0.8` | How far the fog reaches down onto the ground. Larger = thicker low-altitude fog. |

Play with the numbers in the browser console:

```javascript
// Example: make a sunset sky
map.setSky({
  'sky-color': '#ff9e80',
  'horizon-color': '#ffecd2',
  'sky-horizon-blend': 0.6,
  'horizon-fog-blend': 0.5,
  'fog-color': '#ffecd2',
  'fog-ground-blend': 0.4
});
```

---

## 4. Full minimal example (HTML + JS)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – 3D terrain with sky & haze</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        'terrain-dem': {
          type: 'raster-dem',
          url: 'https://tiles.mapterhorn.com/tilejson.json',
          maxzoom: 12
        },
        'sat': {
          type: 'raster',
          url: 'https://tiles.example.com/sat/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
          tileSize: 256
        }
      },
      layers: [
        { id: 'sat', type: 'raster', source: 'sat' }
        // …any other layers you need
      ]
    },
    center: [13.4, 52.5],
    zoom: 12,
    pitch: 70,
    bearing: 0,
    antialias: true
  });

  map.on('load', () => {
    // Enable terrain
    map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1 });

    // Add the sky + haze
    map.setSky({
      'sky-color': '#199EF3',
      'horizon-color': '#ffffff',
      'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,
      'horizon-fog-blend': 0.4,
      'fog-color': '#cce6ff',
      'fog-ground-blend': 0.3
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Open the file in a browser, tilt the map, and you’ll see a blue sky that fades to white at the horizon with a light blue-white haze that clings to the lower slopes.

---

## 5. Common pitfalls (and how to avoid them)

| Symptom | Cause | Fix |
|---------|-------|-----|
| **Sky never appears** | `map.setSky()` called **before** the `load` event, or the map style has `sky: null`. | Call `setSky` inside `map.on('load', …)` or after the style is fully loaded. |
| **Horizon still shows page background** | A **full-screen background layer** with an opaque colour placed **after** the sky (rare, but possible if you manually insert a `background` layer later). | Ensure any `background` layer is defined **before** the sky (or simply remove it). |
| **Fog looks too thick / disappears** | Wrong blend values (`fog-ground-blend` too high or `horizon-fog-blend` = 0). | Adjust the blend numbers; start with the defaults shown above. |
| **Sun / moon direction wanted** | MapLibre does **not** support `sky-atmosphere-sun` or `sky-atmosphere-sun-intensity`. | Use only the colour/gradient properties; for a “sunset” feel, change `sky-color` and `horizon-color` manually. |
| **Performance drops** | Very high `exaggeration` + high zoom + many draped layers. | Keep `exaggeration` modest (1-2), limit the number of raster/hillshade layers, and use `antialias: true` only when needed. |

---

### TL;DR

1. **Enable terrain** (`map.setTerrain({source:'terrain-dem',exaggeration:1})`).  
2. **Add a sky object** – either in the style JSON (`"sky": { … }`) **or** via `map.setSky({ … })`.  
3. Use the sky’s fog-related properties (`fog-color`, `horizon-fog-blend`, `fog-ground-blend`) to create the haze you want.  
4. Make sure you call `setSky` after the map has loaded and that no later layer is covering it.

That’s it – the empty page background above the horizon will be replaced by a proper sky with a gentle atmospheric haze that blends naturally into your

Below is a complete, copy-and-paste-ready recipe for putting a **pre-rendered shaded-relief JPEG tile set** under all of your vector data (including the label layer) in a MapLibre GL JS map.

---

## 1.  Add a *raster* source that points at your JPEG tiles

```js
// 1️⃣  Create the source – replace the URL template with yours
map.addSource('shaded-relief', {
  type: 'raster',
  // Example:  https://my.tileserver.com/relief/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg
  tiles: ['https://my.tileserver.com/relief/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg'],
  tileSize: 256,               // most raster tiles are 256 px
  // optional – if your tiles are in WebP, PNG, etc. just change the extension
  // optional – set a maxzoom if you know it, otherwise omit
  // maxzoom: 14,
  // optional – tell MapLibre how to treat the raster’s colour space
  // (most JPEGs are sRGB, which is the default)
});
```

> **Why `type: 'raster'`?**  
> A pre-rendered image (JPEG/PNG/WebP) is *not* a DEM. It is drawn with a **raster layer**, not a `hillshade` or `color-relief` layer. Those layer types only work with a `raster-dem` source.

---

## 2.  Add a *raster* layer that uses that source

```js
// 2️⃣  Add the layer – put it at the very bottom of the style
map.addLayer(
  {
    id: 'relief-basemap',
    type: 'raster',
    source: 'shaded-relief',
    paint: {
      // Full opacity (you can lower this if you want the vector data to “show through”)
      'raster-opacity': 1.0,
      // Optional: smooth the transition when you zoom/pan
      // 'raster-fade-duration': 300,
    },
  },
  // 👇  Insert it *before* the first symbol layer (labels) so the labels stay on top.
  // If you already know the id of a layer that should be on top, use that id.
  // If you don’t know any ids yet, you can call `map.getStyle().layers` and pick the first
  // layer whose type is "symbol".
  firstSymbolLayerId()
);
```

### Helper: find the first symbol layer (labels)

```js
function firstSymbolLayerId() {
  const layers = map.getStyle().layers;
  for (const l of layers) {
    if (l.type === 'symbol') return l.id;   // the first label layer
  }
  // Fallback – if there are no symbol layers, just add it at the top of the stack
  return undefined;
}
```

*If you already have a known label layer (e.g. `"place-label"`), you can replace the call with that string:*

```js
'place-label'   // <- beforeId argument
```

---

## 3.  Verify the order

After the map finishes loading, you can double-check the order with the console:

```js
console.log(map.getStyle().layers.map(l => `${l.id} (${l.type})`));
```

You should see `relief-basemap` **before** any `symbol` layers (or before the layer you passed as `beforeId`). Anything that comes later (e.g. `line`, `fill`, `symbol`) will be drawn on top of the raster basemap.

---

## 4.  (Optional) Tweak raster rendering

| Property | What it does | Typical values |
|----------|--------------|----------------|
| `raster-opacity` | Transparency of the raster layer | `0.0 – 1.0` |
| `raster-fade-duration` | Fade-in/out time when tiles change (ms) | `0` (no fade) or `300` |
| `raster-resampling` | How the raster is resampled when zoomed | `"linear"` (default) or `"nearest"` |
| `raster-brightness-min` / `raster-brightness-max` | Simple brightness/contrast tweaks | `0 – 1` |
| `raster-hue-rotate`, `raster-saturation` | Color adjustments | degrees / `0 – 1` |

Add any of those to the `paint` object if you need to tone the basemap to match your vector data.

---

## 5.  Full example (HTML + JS)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>Shaded-relief JPEG basemap under vector data</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body { margin:0; padding:0; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        // 1️⃣  Your vector source(s) – e.g. OpenStreetMap data from a vector tile service
        'osm': {
          type: 'vector',
          url: 'https://basemaps.cartocdn.com/rastertiles/voyager/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf' // placeholder
        }
      },
      layers: [
        // 2️⃣  Example vector layer (roads) – you can replace this with your own style
        {
          id: 'road-lines',
          type: 'line',
          source: 'osm',
          'source-layer': 'road',
          paint: { 'line-color': '#555', 'line-width': 2 }
        },
        // 3️⃣  Example label layer – this will stay on top
        {
          id: 'place-label',
          type: 'symbol',
          source: 'osm',
          'source-layer': 'place',
          layout: {
            'text-field': ['get', 'name'],
            'text-size': 12,
            'text-anchor': 'center'
          },
          paint: { 'text-color': '#000' }
        }
      ]
    },
    center: [-122.447303, 37.753574],
    zoom: 12,
    pitch: 45,          // optional – tilt if you also have 3-D terrain later
    bearing: 0
  });

  map.on('load', () => {
    // ---- 1️⃣  Add the raster source (your JPEG tiles) ----
    map.addSource('shaded-relief', {
      type: 'raster',
      tiles: ['https://my.tileserver.com/relief/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg'],
      tileSize: 256
    });

    // ---- 2️⃣  Insert the raster layer under the first symbol layer ----
    const beforeId = (function () {
      const layers = map.getStyle().layers;
      for (const l of layers) {
        if (l.type === 'symbol') return l.id;
      }
      return undefined; // no symbols → just add on top
    })();

    map.addLayer(
      {
        id: 'relief-basemap',
        type: 'raster',
        source: 'shaded-relief',
        paint: { 'raster-opacity': 1.0 }
      },
      beforeId   // <-- ensures labels stay on top
    );
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

*Replace* `https://my.tileserver.com/relief/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg` *with the URL template that serves your JPEG tiles.* The rest of the style (vector sources, roads, labels, etc.) can be whatever you already have – the only thing you need to add is the raster source + layer shown above.

---

## 6.  Quick checklist

| ✅ | Item |
|---|------|
| **Source** | `type: 'raster'`, `tiles: ['…{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg']`, `tileSize: 256` |
| **Layer** | `type: 'raster'`, `source: 'shaded-relief'` |
| **Order** | Insert the raster layer **before** the first `symbol` layer (or any layer you want on top). |
| **Opacity / Styling** | Adjust `raster-opacity`, `raster-fade-duration`, etc., if needed. |
| **No DEM needed** | Because the tiles are already shaded, you don’t use `raster-dem`, `hillshade`, or `color-relief`. |
| **Labels stay on top** | By placing the raster layer low in the stack, all vector `symbol` layers (labels, icons) render above it automatically. |

That’s it! Your beautiful pre-rendered shaded-relief JPEG basemap will now sit snugly beneath all of your vector data, with labels and other symbols rendered on top. 🎉
